{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you — speak, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>and you</b>, which it is not accustomed to say regarding other commandments. Also the addition of the ו״ו. Further, why does it repeat saying <b>Speak to the children of Israel, saying</b> — and although I explained at the beginning of parashat Terumah that it needs to say ״דַּבֵּר״ and ״לֵאמֹר״ alone would not suffice, here it adds the word לֵאמֹר a second time besides the first לֵאמֹר. Further, one needs to know why it says <b>אַךְ</b> (only), and why it says <b>My Sabbaths</b> (שַׁבְּתוֹתַי) in the plural. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded in chapter Kllal Gadol (Shabbat 69a) that it comes to give one observance to many Sabbaths — see there. And in another place (Mekhilta) they expounded ״שַׁבְּתוֹתַי״ to include things that are prohibited by way of shevut (rabbinic rest)." "It appears to me to explain the verse, but first let us establish the intent of the phrase ״אַךְ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי,״ and from there we will raise a path to understand why it says ״and you״ and the addition of ״saying,״ and it will be explained based on what our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 129a) — that one desecrates the Sabbath for the needs of a dangerously ill person, and for all matters of pikuach nefesh (life-threatening situations) and the like. And this is what He hinted by saying <b>אַךְ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתָי</b> — meaning, there exists a circumstance where you are not to observe Sabbath. And scrutinize His saying <b>et My Sabbaths</b>, to include everything called Sabbath — whether the Sabbath of Creation, or Yom HaKippurim, or the other festivals regarding the labors forbidden on them; and also to include even Sabbath after Sabbath: if he is still in danger, one does not say, ״I have already desecrated one Sabbath for him,״ but rather it remains permitted until he emerges from the danger." "It further intends to hint at one matter that our Sages of blessed memory raised (Yoma 84a) (the latter authorities), namely: if they assessed a dangerously ill person and determined that he needs a known treatment that involves Sabbath labor for eight days, one does not say to desecrate only one Sabbath, but rather one performs it immediately, even though this desecrates two Sabbaths — end quote. And this is His saying <b>אַךְ אֶת שַׁבְּתֹתָי</b>." "<b>Further,</b> it hints in His saying <b>et My Sabbaths</b> to give a reason for the diminution (mi'ut): Why do I tell you to desecrate Sabbath for the sick person? This is not called desecration — on the contrary, it is called observance of Sabbath! Desecrate one Sabbath on his behalf so that he may observe many Sabbaths. And the measure of the verse according to this is as follows: Observe many Sabbaths by performing a minimal observance in them, and the way this comes about is by desecrating Sabbath for the sick person — for through this you desecrate one Sabbath in order to observe many Sabbaths. And we do not say in this case ״one does not say to a person: sin so that your fellow may merit,״ for that applies specifically to one who transgresses and desecrates Sabbath of his own accord, as in that which they said (Shabbat 4a): he stuck the loaf in the oven and thereby desecrated Sabbath and is liable to death for this — to him one does not say ״sin,״ etc. But regarding a sick person the Torah said: desecrate! — even David King of Israel, were the matter to come to his hand, would desecrate; and one who refrains from this is nothing other than one who treats a commandment of El with contempt, Heaven forbid." "And it concludes saying <b>for it is a sign between Me and you</b> — meaning, and if so, if there is no observer there is no Sabbath." "And the verse's saying <b>that you may know</b> — here the Holy One, blessed be He, was clever to make known that the holiness of a Jewish soul is greater than the holiness of Shabbat, for this is set aside for that, and it is a simple matter that the lesser is set aside for the greater. And this is what it means by <b>that you may know</b> — meaning, through this you know that I am YHWH who sanctifies you. May His great name be exalted and sanctified:" "<b>And Rashi</b>, of blessed memory, explained ״אַךְ״ as a restriction — the work of the Mishkan is excluded, so that it not be done on Shabbat. And his words are true, except that the implication of the restriction would be: on Shabbat, nothing is excluded from it. Moreover, how would it occur to the mind that they would do the work of the Mishkan on Shabbat, such that it was necessary to restrict? And our intention in this questioning is not to object to or challenge the commentaries, and in particular the words of the holy Rashi — these and those are the words of the living Elohim." "And in our own way, let us seek understanding of the Holy One's intention in saying <b>And you, speak</b> etc. — for the intention is that the Holy One alluded to one restriction in the observance of Shabbat, and as for this restriction, what it entails was not made known in writing, but to Moses He said its explanation orally and not in explicit writing. For our Torah, whose matters are exalted, is refined and its letters are counted, and every single letter declares laws and wonders, as the Master established it with great wisdom in seventy faces and four modes and thirty-one paths, and every path through several trails. Go out and learn from the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 19:3) on the verse ״שְׁלֹשֶׁת אֲלָפִים״ etc. — and all of it in Scripture is minimal. Only you must know that there are derashot and fine analyses in the Torah that are an obligation upon every created being to know, and these are matters that contain laws, and there are some that are not an obligation upon everyone." "And let not your heart take you to speak before Elohim, saying: why would He do thus to His servants, hiding from them mysteries of wisdom and not making known every matter in explicit writing? Behold, your answer is before your eyes — for they are matters that no book could contain. Go out and learn from the statement of our early ones (Avot d'Rabbi Natan 25): they used to say, if the heavens were parchment etc., it would not suffice to write what he learned from his teacher, and he did not lack a drop from the sea — and so too his disciple said of him. And all of it is hinted at straight in Scripture. Great is our Master and abundant in understandings, who wrote a minimal Scripture yet through it conveyed what no creature has the power to write from the day Elohim created man on earth until the end of all generations — and all was transmitted to Moses at Sinai. And therefore, since He stated in writing a restriction regarding the observance of Shabbat, and orally the intention of the restriction was stated to Moses — and this is the Oral Torah — therefore the Holy One commanded Moses to tell Israel the intention of the matter which He had made known to him, to convey it to all the people of Israel, for this matter is an obligation to convey to everyone. And this is what it means by <b>And you</b> — meaning, in addition to what is written, you shall add to speak to them orally the intention of the concealed statement, which is what is restricted in the observance of the Shabbatot." "<b>And this</b> is what it means by <b>Speak to the children of Israel, saying</b> — meaning, speak to them the intention of the statement, what I explained to you concerning it, for the statement itself already says ״לֵאמֹר״, and it refers only to the stating of the explanation told to him orally as mentioned, for this is among the matters that are obligatory for a person to hear. And according to this approach, we have a rule to walk in the paths of life throughout the entire Torah: wherever Scripture says <b>לֵאמֹר</b> and then again says <b>Speak to the children of Israel</b> — one refers to the written matters and one to the oral matters that are needed for the laws which a person is to perform. And although we explained in another manner, these and those are the words of the living Elohim." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to explain the verse's saying <b>But as for you</b>, etc., along the lines of what they said in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 10a) concerning the verse ״לָדַעַת כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״ etc. — that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: ״A precious gift I have in My treasury״ etc. From here [we learn] that one who gives a gift to his fellow must inform him, and the reason is in order to hold favor and grace toward him. And they raised a difficulty from the radiance of Moses' face, and said that his [radiance] was ״עֲבִידָא לְאִגְּלוּיֵי״ (destined to be revealed) etc. — thus far. And this is the verse's saying <b>But as for you, speak</b> — meaning, do not take measure from yourself: when the gift of the horns of splendor is revealed to you and I did not inform you, you would say the same regarding the gift of Shabbat that I am giving to Israel and you would not reveal it — not so! Rather, ״speak״ etc., and do not take the principle from yourself. And the reason is that you are destined to know, but Israel are not עֲבִידָא לְאִגְּלוּיֵי (destined to be revealed) — that they should know on their own that they have it. And the verse's saying <b>it is a sign</b> — meaning: and do not say, is not Shabbat also destined to be revealed, and will they not know on their own that they have rest and delight in it — also the physical recuperation, lest one fall ill from the exertions of labor, and many other particular benefits? Therefore it says <b>for it is a sign</b> — meaning, all the rest and delight that Israel experience on the day of Shabbat is nothing but ״a sign between Me״ etc. — meaning, a sign for the hidden good, which is the aspect of the world that is entirely Shabbat, and it is unattainable except through the mitzvah of Shabbat; and it is the extra soul that comes from it. And understand that this is a hidden sod of Atik. And if so, it is not destined to be revealed; therefore <b>speak</b> etc. <b>that they may know</b> etc. — for it is necessary to inform them of the greatness of the gift, to hold favor toward it and to bless the Giver, blessed be He, who sanctifies them with the holiness of Shabbat to attain the surpassing and wondrous bliss."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {10 "The Aramaic phrases עֲבִידָא לְאִגְּלוּיֵי (lit. 'destined/available for revelation') are rendered with the Aramaic transliterated and a parenthetical gloss, since the argument turns on this terminology from the Talmudic passage. 'Atik' refers to the kabbalistic supernal partzuf."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:40.387850032Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}